+*   **Performance:** Measured on an AMD Athlon 64 X2 Dual 4600+ CPU and NVidia GeForce 7900 GTX GPU, style transfer functions showed comparable performance to regular transfer functions, with some test cases showing slightly better frame rates (e.g., 11.9 fps vs 11.7 fps, 12.8 fps vs 12.5 fps) and others slightly worse (e.g., 9.6 fps vs 10.5 fps) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Allows for intuitive direct manipulation via brushes to paint directly into the 2D transfer function dependent texture [S3]. Incorporates gradient magnitude alongside data value to allow finer control and more sophisticated visualization [S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires computation of gradients and normals. Transfer function design is a difficult iterative procedure [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Stefan Bruckner and Michael E. Gröller in their paper "Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering" [S1][S2, unofficial]. It extends the 1D style transfer function approach to multi-dimensional domains, specifically 2D, to handle attributes like data value and gradient magnitude [S1][S2, unofficial]. The concept of using 2D transfer functions for data value and gradient magnitude was previously explored by Kindlmann and Durkin [S3]. The method is used in illustrative volume rendering to achieve artistic styles while maintaining data fidelity.
